# Device nodes deliberately left UNLABELLED (they fall back to the generic
# "device" type, so any access is denied and shows up by name):
#   /dev/mtgpio     0600 radio:root  -- Stock: (allow radio mtgpio_device (chr_file (ioctl read open)))
#   /dev/RT_Monitor 0660 system:system -- Stock: init, surfaceflinger, system_server
#   /dev/touch      0660 root:system   -- Stock: system_server, system_app
#   /dev/btif       0600 system:system -- no Stock user at all
#   /dev/accdet     0600 root:root   -- the accessory-detect (headset) chardev,
#                   major 242. It DOES have a consumer, unlike the four above:
#                   /vendor/lib/hw/audio.primary.mt6755.so is the only blob in
#                   the image that names it, alongside
#                   /sys/bus/platform/drivers/Accdet_Driver/state. It is still
#                   left unlabelled because DAC refuses it first and a label
#                   would not change that: the node is 0600 root:root and the
#                   audio HAL runs as hal_audio_default (audioserver, not
#                   root), so every open fails at the permission check before
#                   SELinux is consulted. Headset detection on this device runs
#                   through the kernel switch class and uevents, not this node.
#                   Deciding it rather than leaving it undecided: if headset
#                   detect ever regresses, fix the DAC in
#                   rootdir/vendor/ueventd.rc first and only then add a type.
# All five exist on the handset. rootdir/vendor/ueventd.rc sets the DAC for four
# of them and deliberately does NOT for /dev/accdet -- that file's own comment
# (ueventd.rc:45-66) records the measurement with a headset in the port. Nothing
# in this build successfully opens any of the five and the tier 1 capture shows
# no denial for any of them. Labelling a node without a reachable
# consumer only creates a type that looks load-bearing.
#
# Nodes in Stock's file_contexts that DO NOT EXIST on this hardware, verified
# with ls on the handset -- do not port their rules across:
#   /dev/nvram      (Stock nvram_device chr_file half; the blk_file half is
#                    by-name/nvram + by-name/proinfo, which are labelled in
#                    sepolicy/nvram/vendor/file_contexts)
#   /dev/mdp_sync   (Stock mtk_mdp_device, used by its mms and camera domains)
